a towering plume of smoke and ash  filled the sky over saint vincent as la  soufriere  volcano continued to shake the caribbean  island  with violent explosions the cloud rose  15 kilometers into the sky and was  visible from space  the situation is that we're currently  experiencing  a very intense ash fall throughout most  of  mainland vincent we've been experiencing  this since  late yesterday the explosive portion of  of the eruption began yesterday morning  and as we  went into the night last night we had uh  the intensity  and and the amount of ash increased  quite considerably  ash covered houses roads and cars  and the smell of sulfur hung in the air  about sixteen  thousand people have been evacuated from  the so-called red zone  surrounding the volcano this morning  some people fled by coast guard vessels  to safer areas  several cruise ships were also assisting  in the evacuation effort  until recently la soufriere had been  quiet  for decades but in 1902 a severe  eruption  resulted in the deaths of at least a  thousand people  there now some 78 emergency shelters  that are active at this point in time  and the last figure we have is 3000  individuals  living in these shelters at this point  in time  there have been no reports of injuries  or deaths in the latest eruption  but damage to crops and farms is  expected to be severe ash from la  supriere  fell as far away as barbados nearly 200  kilometers from saint vincent  countries including antigua cuba guyana  venezuela and the united states  are sending aid to the island nation  home  to 100 000 people scientists  warned that the eruption could continue  for days  or even weeks rob reynolds al jazeera
 

